\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5 (claude-sonnet-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Taupota (ISO 639-3 tpa) is an Austronesian (Oceanic) language of Milne Bay Province, Papua New Guinea. Sources describe it as a dialect chain, with southern Wa'ema and western Wedau varieties. The Ethnologue figure of about 4,500 speakers dates from 2000--2001, while Joshua Project lists 2,600 for the Taupota (Maivara) people. Very little material was found in search, and what follows is short.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Taupota turned up in search.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Taupota language (overview, classification, dialects): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taupota_language}
\end{itemize}
No dictionary or grammar was found in search results.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item The search snippets report a Wycliffe translation of the Gospel of Mark into Taupota (copyright 2010). The eBible entry is: \url{https://ebible.org/details.php?id=tpa}
\item Joshua Project profile of Taupota (Maivara): \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/15291}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network, Taupota entry (audio recordings; notes that speakers also understand Police Motu and Wedau): \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/tpa}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Taupota is not known to be taught in schools, and no educational materials were found. Wedau, which Anglican missionaries used as a lingua franca on the southern coast of Goodenough Bay, and Police Motu are the likely regional contact languages. Any curriculum would need new materials developed with the community. This dossier is short because little was found.
\end{document}
